WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.291
A conditional flaw draws more than the stated relationship permits.

2
00:00:04.591 --> 00:00:12.911
Two common errors are inferring the sufficient condition from the result and inferring the missing result from the missing sufficient condition.

3
00:00:13.211 --> 00:00:15.429
Every selected proposal is reviewed.

4
00:00:15.729 --> 00:00:17.393
This proposal was reviewed.

5
00:00:17.693 --> 00:00:19.208
Therefore it was selected.

6
00:00:19.508 --> 00:00:24.052
The author knows the result and treats it as proof of the sufficient condition.

7
00:00:24.352 --> 00:00:30.069
A reviewed but unselected proposal would satisfy the premise and contradict the conclusion.

8
00:00:30.369 --> 00:00:32.652
That possible case explains the flaw.

9
00:00:32.952 --> 00:00:40.824
It is not necessary to know why a different proposal might be reviewed; the premise never limited review to selected proposals.

10
00:00:41.124 --> 00:00:46.713
A specific counterexample connects abstract flaw descriptions to the argument in front of you.

11
00:00:47.013 --> 00:00:50.213
It also shows why plausible reasoning can still fail.

12
00:00:50.513 --> 00:00:52.796
Write the actual rule and the case fact.

13
00:00:53.096 --> 00:00:55.805
Then identify the conclusion the author adds.

14
00:00:56.105 --> 00:00:58.857
Do not silently supply a missing reverse premise.

15
00:00:59.157 --> 00:01:02.656
Explain the extra inference using this argument’s nouns.

16
00:01:02.956 --> 00:01:08.225
A conditional relationship need not be causal, and an obligation need not be obeyed.

17
00:01:08.525 --> 00:01:15.395
A correct flaw description must identify the actual added claim, not simply mention sufficient and necessary.
